+# BLOCKED: `test_constant_memory` (exact median in O(1) memory)
+
+Date: 2026-10-18
+File: `stats_stream.py`, test: `test_stats_stream.py::MedianTests::test_constant_memory`
+
+## Status
+
+| Item | State |
+| --- | --- |
+| `median([1, 2, 3, 4])` returning `3` | **Fixed** — now returns `2.5` (`stats_stream.py:11-14`) |
+| `test_odd`, `test_even`, `test_empty_raises`, `test_generator_input` | **Pass** |
+| `test_constant_memory` | **BLOCKED — the requirement is unsatisfiable as written** |
+
+The test asks for two things that cannot both hold:
+
+1. `self.assertEqual(got, want)` — the result must be the *bit-exact* median of 200,000
+   arbitrary `double` samples (`test_stats_stream.py:37`).
+2. `self.assertLess(peak, 64 * 1024)` — the call must hold **no more than 64 KB** while doing
+   it (`test_stats_stream.py:38`).
+
+## Why this is impossible
+
+### 1. Exact one-pass selection has a linear-space lower bound
+
+Computing the exact median of a stream whose order is not known in advance requires remembering
+the elements: with an adversarially ordered stream of distinct values, any deterministic one-pass
+algorithm that must be exact needs Ω(n) space (Munro & Paterson, *Selection and sorting with
+limited storage*, 1980). A sub-linear-memory algorithm can always be fooled by re-ordering the
+input, because the median of a set is not determined by any bounded-size summary of it.
+
+`median()` additionally receives a **one-shot iterator** (`feed()` is a generator,
+`test_stats_stream.py:8-10`), so multi-pass workarounds are off the table by the API contract
+itself.
+
+The only bounded-memory algorithms for this problem are **approximate** (P² / Jain–Chlamtac,
+Greenwald–Khanna, reservoir sampling, t-digest). They cannot return a bit-exact double, so they
+fail assertion 1.
+
+### 2. The budget is smaller than the raw payload, before any algorithm runs
+
+64 KB / 200,000 samples = **0.33 bytes per sample**. A `double` is 8 bytes; a CPython `float`
+object is 24 bytes plus an 8-byte list slot.
+
+Measured on this machine (Python 3.12.5, `tracemalloc`, same `feed()` input):
+
+| Representation | Peak traced memory | vs 64 KB budget |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Current exact implementation (`sorted()` copy) | 7,054.9 KB (6.89 MB) | **110x over** |
+| Absolute floor: `array('d')` holding the 200k doubles, nothing else | 1,655.1 KB | **26x over** |
+| Budget | 64.0 KB | — |
+
+So even a perfect algorithm that stored each sample in the most compact form Python offers
+would be ~26x over budget. The gap is not an implementation inefficiency; it is the data.
+
+### 3. Anything that would "pass" is a workaround, not a solution
+
+These were considered and deliberately **not** implemented:
+
+- Spilling chunks to a temp file and doing an external merge sort. It satisfies the
+  `tracemalloc` assertion, but it is not O(1) memory — it is O(n) *state* moved to disk, with
+  unbounded temp-space use, I/O amplification, cleanup/failure handling, and worse throughput on
+  exactly the large feeds that are OOMing today.
+- Keying a cache on the iterable or the RNG seed, or detecting the test's `feed()` twice.
+  That would only satisfy this test, not the production requirement.
+- Returning an approximate median from `median()` while the docstring and test promise an exact
+  one. That silently changes the API contract and would corrupt latency metrics.
+
+## What is needed to unblock (pick one)
+
+- **A. Keep exactness, accept O(n) memory (recommended for correctness):** drop or relax
+  `test_constant_memory`. If the OOM risk still matters, the memory constant can be cut ~4x by
+  buffering into `array('d')` and running an in-place quickselect instead of `sorted()`
+  (~1.6 MB per 200k samples, measured above) — still O(n), but far from today's 6.9 MB.
+- **B. Keep constant memory, relax exactness:** add a separate streaming estimator (P² or
+  Greenwald–Khanna, a handful of KB, deterministic or randomised) and assert with a tolerance,
+  e.g. `assertAlmostEqual(got, want, delta=0.01 * want)`. Roughly 0.1–1% relative error at
+  O(1) memory. Note that P² degrades on sorted/monotone streams, so GK is the safer choice if
+  feeds can arrive ordered.
+- **C. Both, with distinct names:** keep `median()` exact and add `approx_median()` (or
+  `median(..., approx=True)`) for the high-volume path, documenting the error bound.
+- **D. Exact + O(1) memory — only if the contract changes:** exact selection over IEEE-754
+  doubles is possible with O(1) memory by binary-searching the 64-bit ordered value space over
+  the data (64 counting passes, no storage). This requires a re-iterable source, which a
+  generator is not, and costs 64 full reads of the feed.
+
+## Verification
+
+```
+python3 -m unittest test_stats_stream -v
+# 4 pass; test_constant_memory fails on assertLess(peak, 64 * 1024) only:
+# AssertionError: 7221896 not less than 65536
+```
+
+The value assertion inside `test_constant_memory` now passes (the even-count bug is fixed and the
+median matches the exact reference); only the impossible memory bound remains.
